overs (1,2),(2,1)), x+y=4 (covers (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)). So we can choose any of these as the non-sunny line.\n\nIf we choose x+y=4 (covers (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)). Then we need to cover (1,1),(1,2),(2,1) with two sunny lines. As we saw earlier, any line covering two of these points is either vertical/horizontal or slope -1. Let's check all pairs:\n\n- (1,1) and (1,2): vertical, not allowed.\n- (1,1) and (2,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (1,2) and (2,1): slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we need three sunny lines, not possible.\n\nIf we choose x+y=3 (covers (1,2),(2,1)). Then we need to cover (1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1) with two sunny lines. That's 4 points. Let's see if we can cover them with two sunny lines.\n\nPoints: (1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1). Check possible pairs:\n\n- (1,1) and (2,2): slope 1, allowed. So line y=x covers (1,1),(2,2). Good.\n- (1,3) and (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair them.\n- (1,3) and (2,2): slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (1,3) and (1,1): vertical, not allowed.\n- (3,1) and (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,1) and (1,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus we cannot cover (1,3) and (3,1) with a sunny line. So we need a third sunny line. So fails.\n\nIf we choose x+y=2 (covers (1,1)). Then we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1) with two sunny lines. That's 5 points. Likely impossible.\n\nThus diagonal non-sunny line fails.\n\nCase B: non-sunny line is horizontal y=c. For n=3, possible y values are 1,2,3.\n\nIf y=1 (covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1)). Then we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,2) with two sunny lines. As we saw earlier, we cannot cover (1,2) and (2,2) with a sunny line (horizontal). (1,2) and (1,3) vertical. (1,3) and (2,2) slope -1. So need three sunny lines. So fails.\n\nIf y=2 (covers (1,2),(2,2)). Then we need to cover (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1) with two sunny lines. That's 4 points. Let's see if we can pair them.\n\n- (1,1) and (2,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- 